          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"gissing-the-odd-women-912b570ed81fd9e9\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.98,\"analysis\":\"Rhoda directly avows that she loves Everard, with an epistemic qualification about her understanding of love. The utterance answers his question about her emotional state. It does not itself undertake a commitment, and the surrounding passion and trembling do not establish an independently expressive/reflexive force.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The conditional phrase qualifies Rhoda’s conceptual certainty about love, but does not materially obscure the utterance’s dominant truth-conditional force.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Rhoda directly says “If I understand what love means, I love you” to Everard in response to his asking whether she doubts her own love.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Rhoda sincerely presents her love as real while remaining uncertain about the practical commitment Everard demands. Her refusal to decide immediately, kiss him, or accept the symbolic ring separates this avowal of feeling from an undertaking to marry or enter his proposed union. The narration confirms her passion, but that intensity is contextual evidence and does not make the words a reflex-like discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Everard asks, “Or are you doubtful of your own love?” Rhoda replies, “No. If I understand what love means, I love you.”\",\"supports\":\"The utterance explicitly answers a question about whether Rhoda possesses a loving emotional state, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narration states that Rhoda “loved with passion” and had been allowing herself to indulge that emotion.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms that the avowed loving state is narratively presented as genuine, while not changing its truth-conditional mode.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately afterward Rhoda refuses the requested kiss until she is sure of her readiness, and she removes the marriage ring Everard places on her finger.\",\"supports\":\"Shows that her declaration of love is distinct from assent to the proposed relational or marital undertaking, weighing against P.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Her broken words, trembling, and struggle with passion occur around the exchange, but the declaration itself is a composed, qualified answer to Everard’s question.\",\"supports\":\"Provides emotional intensity without positive evidence that saying the words is an expressive or reflex-like discharge; E remains unsupported.\",\"confidence\":0.95}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core utterance as a qualified avowal of love. Rhoda’s passion, hesitation, and conflict over marriage are adequately treated as context; no residual core force requires P, E, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
